<p class="lead">
  Paste the same paragraph into Word, Google Docs, and a browser-based word counter, and you can sometimes get three slightly different numbers. None of them are wrong, exactly — they're just counting by marginally different rules.
</p>

<h2>Count in your browser (free, instant)</h2>
<p>
  The <a href="/tools/word-counter/">Word Counter</a> on keptlocal counts words, characters, sentences, paragraphs, and estimated reading time as you type — nothing is sent anywhere.
</p>

<h2>What actually counts as a "word"</h2>
<p>
  It sounds like it should be unambiguous, but word counting requires a definition, and different tools quietly choose slightly different ones. The most common convention — and the one this tool uses — treats a word as a sequence of characters separated by whitespace or line breaks. That produces a few results worth knowing:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hyphenated words count as one.</strong> "well-known," "twenty-five," and "state-of-the-art" are each one word, not two or four, because there's no whitespace inside them.</li>
  <li><strong>Numbers count as words.</strong> "2026" and "42" each count as one word, same as any other token.</li>
  <li><strong>Em-dashes and en-dashes can split or not split words</strong> depending on whether whitespace surrounds them — "word—word" (no spaces) is one token in some counters, two in others that treat the dash itself as a separator.</li>
  <li><strong>URLs and email addresses</strong> usually count as a single word despite containing dots and slashes, since there's no whitespace breaking them up.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Microsoft Word and Google Docs use similar rules but aren't identical to each other or to this tool in every edge case — which is why a 2,000-word document might show 1,998 in one tool and 2,003 in another. The difference is almost always in how hyphens, numbers, or special punctuation get tokenized, not a bug in either tool.
</p>

<h2>Where reading time estimates come from</h2>
<p>
  The reading-time figure — "6 min read," commonly shown on articles including this one — comes from dividing word count by an assumed reading speed, typically 200–250 words per minute for adult silent reading of general-audience text. This tool uses 238 wpm, a figure from published reading-speed research. It's a population average, not a prediction for any specific reader: technical or unfamiliar material reads slower, skimming reads faster, and reading speed varies meaningfully between individuals. Treat reading-time estimates as a rough sense of an article's length, not a countdown timer.
</p>

<h2>Common places word and character limits actually matter</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Social platforms:</strong> X/Twitter posts (280 characters), LinkedIn posts (3,000 characters), Instagram captions (2,200 characters) — all counted in characters, not words.</li>
  <li><strong>Academic and professional writing:</strong> essay word limits, abstract limits (often 150–300 words), cover letter guidance (typically under 400 words).</li>
  <li><strong>SEO meta descriptions:</strong> roughly 150–160 characters before search engines truncate the display.</li>
  <li><strong>Forms and applications:</strong> many online forms enforce hard character limits on text fields — knowing your count before pasting avoids losing content to truncation.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Privacy: what happens to your text</h2>
<p>
  Counting happens locally in your browser as you type — nothing is transmitted, logged, or stored. This matters for anything you wouldn't want passing through a third-party server before it's finished: an unpublished draft, a confidential report, a cover letter with personal details.
</p>

